---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import register from '../data/register.json';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const c = register.counts;
---
<Base title="The Luwinski Archive"
  subtitle="An evidence-first archive for the family of Lynn Derrick Luwinski — Berlin, Schubin, Sauerbaum, Lourenço Marques, Johannesburg, Porto."
  lede="Four of this family's men were deported from Berlin between 1941 and 1944 and did not come back. One young man got out in 1933 and became a dress designer in Johannesburg. A woman born in Portuguese East Africa married four times in that city and was buried in Porto. This archive sets down what can be documented about all of them — and one finding that unsettles how they are joined together.">

  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.family}</b><span>People in the family proper</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>4</b><span>In the Gedenkbuch</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.index}</b><span>Surname-index entries, not descent</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{c.omitted_living}</b><span>Living people omitted</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Two spines</h2>
  <p>
    <a href={base + '/derrick/'}>Lynn Derrick Luwinski</a> is living, and this archive names him and
    says nothing else about him. Behind him run <strong>two lines, and they are not the same line.</strong>
    One is the family of record, documented to the 1870s and carried on a name. The other is the family
    his DNA keeps pointing at, reconstructed from 5,464 matches. As of <strong>15 September 2026</strong>
    that second lane has names on it all the way down, and the lean has become steep: the shared segments
    place him inside a Hertfordshire household called <strong>Wear</strong>, and a man of that family was
    living in Johannesburg in 1948. Both are still set down side by side, because <strong>no document yet
    joins the second one to him</strong> — only the arithmetic does.
  </p>

  <div class="spines">
    <div class="gen head"></div>
    <div class="node head a">
      <b>The line of record</b> <span class="tag doc">Documented</span>
      <p>Paper, from Berlin and the Posen–West Prussia borderland. The family Derrick was born into, named for, and belongs to.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="node head b">
      <b>The line the DNA points to</b> <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span>
      <p>Everyone here is documented — English registers, South African registers, a court index. What is <em>not</em> documented is the last joint, the one to Derrick.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="gen">Great-<br/>grandparents</div>
    <div class="node a">
      <span class="lane">Line of record</span>
      <b>Sally Leibholz &amp; Friederike Schmul</b>
      <p>Hammerstein; married at Szubin, 1874. A Jewish family of the borderland.</p>
      <b>Joachim Luwinski &amp; Johanna Thimm</b>
      <p>Allenstein and Sauerbaum, East Prussia. Catholic farming people of the Ermland.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="node b">
      <span class="lane">DNA line</span>
      <b>George Castle <span class="yr">b. Baldock 1863</span> &amp; Caroline Wallis <span class="yr">b. Weston 1863</span></b>
      <p>
        Married 1883. Farm labourer, later a labourer in the Baldock brewery. Hertfordshire —
        <strong>not Norfolk</strong>, an error this archive carried for weeks.
      </p>
      <b>The Bermondsey side</b>
      <p>
        <strong>Henry Robert Wear</strong>, born Bermondsey, London, 1889 — a printer&rsquo;s machine
        minder at <strong>J. M. Dent &amp; Sons</strong>, Letchworth. His own parents are not yet found.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="gen">Grandparents</div>
    <div class="node a">
      <span class="lane">Line of record</span>
      <b><a href={base + '/leibholz/'}>Jacob Leibholz</a> <span class="yr">b. Schubin 1884</span></b>
      <p>Deported from Berlin to Auschwitz, 26 February 1943. Did not come back.</p>
      <b><a href={base + '/east-prussia/'}>Auguste “Lizzy” Luwinski</a> <span class="yr">1893–1975</span></b>
      <p>Dressmaker and milliner. Survived the war in Berlin.</p>
      <div class="sibs"><span>Jacob&rsquo;s brothers</span>
        <strong>Samuel</strong> <span class="yr">1877&ndash;1943</span> &middot;
        <strong>Georg</strong> <span class="yr">1884&ndash;1942</span> &middot;
        <strong>Oskar</strong> <span class="yr">1887&ndash;1944</span> — <em>all four brothers are in the Gedenkbuch. None came back.</em>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="node b open">
      <span class="lane">DNA line</span>
      <span class="lane">DNA line</span>
      <b>Henry Robert Wear &amp; Martha Maud Castle <span class="tag inf">Named, 15 September</span></b>
      <p>
        Married at <strong>Hitchin</strong>, last quarter of <strong>1914</strong> — the only
        Wear&ndash;Castle marriage in the whole English index. By 1921 at <strong>72 Pix Road,
        Letchworth</strong>, with four children and a lodger.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>And they went too.</strong> He died in Johannesburg on <strong>11 September 1969</strong>,
        aged 80, and is buried at West Park; she died there on <strong>16 March 1986</strong>, aged 90.
        The whole household crossed.
      </p>
      <p class="verdict inf">
        <strong>The segments name them as a couple, not a line.</strong> Of the 297.5 cM Derrick shares
        with his closest match, only ~44 falls in Castle territory; <strong>~253 cM lies outside every
        region he shares with any Castle cousin</strong>. Two ancestors in common, not one.
      </p>
      <div class="sibs"><span>Martha&rsquo;s brothers and sisters</span>
        <strong>George W.</strong> 1885 &middot;
        <strong>Ada</strong> 1887 &middot;
        <strong>Frederick</strong> 1890 &middot;
        <strong>Agnes Kate</strong> <span class="yr">1891&ndash;1967</span> — <em>married Walter George Brown; the 292.7 and 333.9 cM matches both descend from her</em> &middot;
        <strong>Hilda</strong> 1902 &middot;
        <strong>Ernest</strong> 1904
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gen">Father</div>
    <div class="node a">
      <span class="lane">Line of record</span>
      <b><a href={base + '/kurt/'}>Kurt Israel Luwinski</a> <span class="yr">b. Berlin 1912, d. Johannesburg 1968</span></b>
      <p>
        Got out of Germany in 1933, aged 21. Dress designer; partner in the Piccadilly Hat Company,
        81 Eloff Street. Married Ivone in Johannesburg, June 1948.
      </p>
      <p class="verdict disp">Very probably <strong>not</strong> the biological father: 0.0% Ashkenazi where a quarter is required, and a Y-DNA haplogroup — R-S11477, U106 — that is North Sea, not Ashkenazi.</p>
      <div class="sibs"><span>His brother</span>
        <strong>Siegfried Leibholz</strong> <span class="yr">1925&ndash;2005</span> — born after his parents married, and registered a <em>Leibholz</em> where Kurt stayed a <em>Luwinski</em>.
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="node b open">
      <span class="lane">DNA line</span>
      <span class="lane">DNA line</span>
      <b><a href={base + '/dna/#wear'}>Leonard Aubrey Robert Wear</a> <span class="yr">b. 1917</span></b>
      <p>
        Born at <strong>Hitchin</strong>, on the 1921 census at Letchworth aged four — and then absent
        from every English record there is. He was in <strong>Johannesburg</strong>, where he married
        <strong>Phyllis Agnes Robertson at Rosebank on 7 September 1940</strong>, was divorced by her in
        <strong>1951</strong>, and married again that October.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Thirty-one in June 1948</strong>, in the right city, married to somebody else. And he is
        why his sister <strong>Cynthia</strong> was in Johannesburg to marry there in 1952 — her brother
        had been there twelve years.
      </p>
      <p class="verdict inf">
        <strong>And there were only two sons.</strong> The birth index, searched on the mother&rsquo;s
        maiden name, gives the couple <strong>six children</strong> — all at Hitchin, 1917 to 1936 —
        of whom two were boys. <strong>In June 1948 one was thirty-one and the other was eleven.</strong>
      </p>
      <div class="sibs"><span>His brother and sisters</span>
        <strong>Janette E M</strong> 1919 &middot;
        <strong>Kathleen E</strong> 1921 &middot;
        <strong>Cynthia Y E</strong> 1931 — <em>married Mervin Halsey at Johannesburg in 1952; her granddaughter is the 297.5 cM match</em> &middot;
        <strong>Linda A</strong> 1933 &middot;
        <strong>Bruce Wallace</strong> 1936 — <em>married at Kuruman, Cape Province, 1959</em>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gen">Mothers</div>
    <div class="sibrow">
      <div class="node sib span2 shared">
        <span class="rel">Mother of <em>Henrique</em> and <em>Derrick</em></span>
        <b><a href={base + '/ivone/'}>Ivone <strong>Mota</strong> Figueiredo</a></b>
        <span class="yr">b. Lourenço Marques 1924, d. Porto</span>
        <p>
          <strong>Not in dispute on either reading.</strong> Four marriages, three countries. Whichever
          man fathered Derrick, she was his mother — which is also why the Portuguese half of his DNA is
          the half that behaves. Henrique is hers by the first of those marriages, to a da Rocha.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="node b open sib">
        <span class="rel">Mother of <em>Edwina</em>? <span class="tag open">If Edwina is his</span></span>
        <b>Phyllis Agnes Robertson</b>
        <span class="yr">b. 1915 &middot; married Leonard 1940, divorced him 1951</span>
        <p>
          <strong>No relation to Derrick whatsoever</strong>, and not his mother by any reading. She is
          here because <strong>Edwina&rsquo;s mother is not Ivone</strong> — if Edwina is Leonard&rsquo;s
          daughter, this is whose daughter she is, and Derrick would share a father with her and nothing
          else.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="gen">His generation</div>
    <div class="sibrow">
      <div class="node a sib">
        <span class="rel">Half-brother &middot; shared <em>mother</em></span>
        <b>Henrique Figueiredo Pinto da Rocha</b>
        <span class="yr">11 March 1941 &ndash; 27 October 2022</span>
        <p>
          Father: <strong>a da Rocha</strong>, Ivone&rsquo;s first husband, whom the family tree can
          only call <em>Unknown</em>. <strong>Derrick&rsquo;s brother on either reading</strong>, because
          the dispute has only ever been about <em>his</em> father. Estate <strong>12294/2023</strong> —
          the file that would finally name the da Rocha.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="node derrick sib">
        <span class="rel">The person this archive is about</span>
        <b><a href={base + '/derrick/'}>Lynn Derrick Luwinski</a></b>
        <p>Living. Named here, and described nowhere.</p>
        <p class="verdict inf">
          <strong>Nobody on the Luwinski side.</strong> Kurt married three times and
          <strong>one child is recorded across all three</strong>. Both of the people either side of
          him here reach him through a different parent.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="node b open sib">
        <span class="rel">Half-sister? &middot; shared <em>father</em> <span class="tag open">Unproven</span></span>
        <b>Edwina Ann Wear</b>
        <span class="yr">1945 &ndash; 30 June 2014</span>
        <p>
          Buried at Braamfontein, Johannesburg. If she is Leonard&rsquo;s, then her father is his and
          <strong>her mother is Phyllis, not Ivone</strong> — she would be a half-sister through the
          father alone. <strong>Her parents are not recorded</strong> in anything found, so for now she
          is a coincidence with good timing.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>



    <div class="gen"></div>
    <div class="note both">
      <strong>What would turn the right-hand lane from a strong guess into a fact:</strong>
      <strong>WLD 2026/1951</strong> and <strong>WLD 790/1957</strong> in the National Archives at
      Pretoria — a divorce, and six years later an opposed application against the same woman, which in
      that court usually means children. Or a living male-line Wear taking a Y test, whose result should
      be Derrick&rsquo;s. <a href={base + '/dna/#wear'}>How the segments got here, and what is still
      only inferred &rarr;</a>
    </div>
  </div>


  <h2>The finding this archive cannot avoid</h2>
  <div class="panel wide">
    <h3>Kurt Israel Luwinski is very probably not Derrick's biological father <span class="tag inf">Inferred</span></h3>
    <p>If he were, Derrick's DNA would carry about a quarter Ashkenazi Jewish from Jacob Leibholz and
    about a quarter East Prussian German from Auguste. It carries <strong>0.0%</strong> and
    <strong>1.4%</strong>. His nearest matches are British and Portuguese, and there is no German or
    Jewish cluster anywhere near the top of a list 5,464 long.</p>
    <p><strong>And the Y-DNA now agrees.</strong> Derrick's paternal haplogroup, read on 14 September
    2026, is <strong>R-S11477</strong> — R1b, branch <strong>U106/Z381</strong>, the North Sea lineage
    that peaks in the Netherlands and England. It is not a lineage characteristic of Ashkenazi paternal
    descent. Two independent systems, the same answer, and they agree on direction as well: the
    autosomal cluster is British, and the haplogroup's largest European population is England.</p>
    <p class="small"><a href={base + '/dna/'}>The evidence, the arithmetic, what the Y can and cannot prove →</a></p>
  </div>
  <p class="wide">
    That does not make the Berlin history untrue, and it does not make it less his. Jacob Leibholz died
    at Auschwitz; his brothers Samuel and Oskar died there too, and Georg at Chełmno. Kurt escaped in
    1933 and carried the name. It is the family Derrick was born into and named for. Whether it is also
    the family he descends from is a different question, and this archive keeps the two apart rather
    than pretending they are one.
  </p>


<style>
  .spines {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 7.5rem 1fr 1fr;
    gap: .5rem;
    margin: 1.8rem 0 1rem;
    max-width: none;
    align-items: stretch;
  }
  .spines .gen {
    font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .07em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-faint);
    padding: .85rem .2rem 0 0;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }
  .spines .node {
    background: var(--panel);
    border: 1px solid var(--rule);
    border-radius: 3px;
    padding: .8rem .95rem;
    max-width: none;
    font-size: .88rem;
  }
  .spines .node.a { border-left: 3px solid var(--doc); }
  .spines .node.b { border-left: 3px solid var(--inf); }
  .spines .node.b.open { border-left-style: dashed; border-left-color: var(--open); }
  .spines .node.both { grid-column: 2 / span 2; border-left: 3px solid var(--rule); }
  .spines .node.derrick { border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); }
  .spines .node.head { background: transparent; border: 0; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--rule); border-radius: 0; padding: 0 0 .5rem; }
  .spines .node b { display: block; font-weight: 600; margin-top: .55rem; line-height: 1.35; }
  .spines .node b:first-of-type { margin-top: 0; }
  .spines .node p { margin: .2rem 0 0; font-size: .84rem; color: var(--ink-soft); max-width: none; }
  .spines .yr { font-weight: 400; color: var(--ink-faint); font-size: .82em; white-space: nowrap; }
  .spines .verdict { margin-top: .55rem; padding-top: .5rem; border-top: 1px dotted var(--rule); font-size: .8rem; }
  .spines .verdict.disp { color: var(--disp); }
  .spines .verdict.inf { color: var(--ink-soft); }
  .spines .lane { display: none; }
  .spines .sibrow {
    grid-column: 2 / span 2;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr 1fr;
    gap: .5rem;
    max-width: none;
  }
  .spines .sib .rel {
    display: block;
    font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: .6rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-faint); margin-bottom: .45rem;
  }
  .spines .sib .rel em { font-style: normal; color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .spines .sib > .yr { display: block; margin-top: .1rem; }
  .spines .span2 { grid-column: 1 / span 2; }
  .spines .node.shared { border-left: 3px solid var(--rule); }
  .spines .sibs {
    margin-top: .6rem; padding-top: .55rem;
    border-top: 1px dotted var(--rule);
    font-size: .79rem; line-height: 1.5; color: var(--ink-soft);
  }
  .spines .sibs > span:first-child {
    display: block;
    font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
    font-size: .58rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--ink-faint); margin-bottom: .25rem;
  }
  .spines .sibs strong { color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600; }
  .spines .sibs em { font-style: italic; }
  .spines .note {
    grid-column: 2 / span 2;
    max-width: none;
    font-size: .85rem;
    color: var(--ink-soft);
    padding: .85rem 0 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
    margin-top: .3rem;
  }
  .spines .note strong { color: var(--ink); }
  /* this section argues across two columns — it must not be squeezed to a reading measure */
  .wide { max-width: none; }
  .wide p, .wide ul, .wide li { max-width: none; }
  @media (max-width: 52rem) {
    .spines { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: .45rem; }
    .spines .sibrow { grid-column: auto; grid-template-columns: 1fr; }
    .spines .span2 { grid-column: auto; }
    .spines .gen { padding: 1.1rem 0 0; border-top: 1px solid var(--rule); }
    .spines .gen.head { display: none; }
    .spines .node.head { display: none; }
    .spines .node.both { grid-column: auto; }
    .spines .lane {
      display: block;
      font-family: ui-sans-serif, system-ui, sans-serif;
      font-size: .6rem; letter-spacing: .1em; text-transform: uppercase;
      color: var(--ink-faint); margin-bottom: .4rem;
    }
  }
</style>

  <h2>Where to start</h2>
  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card"><a href={base + '/leibholz/'}>The Leibholz brothers</a><p>Four men, four entries in the German Federal Archives' Memorial Book. Two corrections to the family tree fall out of them.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><a href={base + '/ivone/'}>Ivone</a><p>Born at Lourenço Marques in 1924. Married a da Rocha, a de Witt, a Luwinski and a Salkow. Buried at Porto.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><a href={base + '/1948/'}>The year 1948</a><p>Two marriages four and a half months apart, and a divorce filed before the child was born.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><a href={base + '/east-prussia/'}>Sauerbaum and Allenstein</a><p>The Catholic half: a farmer, a milliner, and a son born twelve years before his parents married.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><a href={base + '/register/'}>The register</a><p>Every person in the source, labelled — including the hundred who are a surname index and not ancestry.</p></div>
    <div class="card"><a href={base + '/method/'}>Method</a><p>What “documented”, “inferred” and “disputed” mean here, and why living people are absent.</p></div>
  </div>
</Base>
